xpanded_url":"http://www.omniscape.com","indices":[0,23],"url":"https://t.co/WvHWJY0HO2"}]}}},"isAutomated":false,"automatedBy":null},"extendedEntities":{},"card":null,"place":{},"entities":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"timestamps":[],"urls":[],"user_mentions":[{"id_str":"1595502771351920640","indices":[0,8],"name":"Loris","screen_name":"0xLoris"}]},"quoted_tweet":null,"retweeted_tweet":null,"isLimitedReply":false,"communityInfo":null,"article":null},{"type":"tweet","id":"2047913088775491797","url":"https://x.com/0xLoris/status/2047913088775491797","twitterUrl":"https://twitter.com/0xLoris/status/2047913088775491797","text":"@noahlh i see you are a rare coin dealer (and fellow ihtfp enjoyer!)\n\ntbh i think that is a tougher market for this approach since coins are more readily identified by specific markers, and graded. just structurally going to be more efficient (although yes still probably the occasional mispricing)\n\nbut by way of example, how would someone with a fancy cup identify its origin? to them it's just a fancy cup. what do they google, 'fancy cup with fruit on it'? top result is a Kohl's mug for $19. possible to identify, just less likely. someone with low tech IQ might just list it in a lot with other items, or misidentified by itself\n\nwhen in reality, it's a  from a ceremonial serving cup from a russian csar's coronation in the 1700's, and worth $2,000. claude can identify it because it's been trained on thousands of images of russian coronation cups (i actually sold one of these on ebay back in the day). \n\ni think the thesis is intact, but i agree still many landmines. and probably need to write off all specialist dealers, and maybe entire categories (watches made after ~1980, coins, cards)","source":"Twitter for iPhone","retweetCount":0,"replyCount":0,"likeCount":2,"quoteCount":0,"viewCount":66,"createdAt":"Sat Apr 25 05:38:23 +0000 2026","lang":"en","bookmarkCount":0,"isReply":true,"inReplyToId":"2047879890070831273","conversationId":"2047731